      "id": 1,
      "text": "Salt Lick received its name in 1884, after nearby saline springs along Salt Lick Creek fed the town’s early settlers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/salt_lick_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first post office opened in 1882 as Vail, then changed to Salt Lick in 1888 when the town incorporated.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_Lick%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Salt Lick was laid out in 1881 as a station on the Elizabethtown, Lexington and Big Sandy Railroad, later Chesapeake and Ohio.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/salt_lick_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Native Americans used the area’s natural salt licks for centuries, making it a hunting hub before European arrival.",
